WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.700
[SOUND]
[LAUGH]

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.076
&gt;&gt; Okay Brad, you got this.

00:00:08.076 --> 00:00:11.408
Karen, you got your power post its, you
got your blue bottled coffee just don't

00:00:11.408 --> 00:00:13.522
spill it on Marc Benioff or
you're getting fired.

00:00:13.522 --> 00:00:16.603
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Hi everyone.

00:00:16.603 --> 00:00:17.876
I am Brad Hungerman.

00:00:17.876 --> 00:00:21.163
I am the new product manager
here at Salesforce and

00:00:21.163 --> 00:00:26.249
I'm just really excited today to be
talking to you about some of the different

00:00:26.249 --> 00:00:30.883
customer features that we've been
studying for the last few months.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:35.703
We came up with some really good
ideas that I want to share with you.

00:00:35.703 --> 00:00:38.346
And so let me just throw a couple
of stats up on the board here.

00:00:38.346 --> 00:00:39.444
&gt;&gt; What's going on?

00:00:39.444 --> 00:00:41.011
What's going on here?

00:00:41.011 --> 00:00:41.883
&gt;&gt; Is this guy joking?

00:00:41.883 --> 00:00:42.961
&gt;&gt; I don't have time for this.

00:00:42.961 --> 00:00:43.841
&gt;&gt; Should we talk to his?

00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:44.958
&gt;&gt; Now why is he speaking here?

00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:46.405
&gt;&gt; Why is he so nervous?

00:00:46.405 --> 00:00:47.835
&gt;&gt; Guys, I have to go.

00:00:47.835 --> 00:00:51.440
&gt;&gt; Well, well,
I can't remember the exact statistics but

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.380
the really important
thing that I want you to.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.703
Where'd everyone go?

00:00:56.703 --> 00:01:00.951
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I want to ask you,

00:01:00.951 --> 00:01:02.623
what did Brad do wrong here?

00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:04.682
Anyone?

00:01:07.384 --> 00:01:08.429
Stephanie?

00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:14.387
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; You got it right.

00:01:14.387 --> 00:01:17.250
He was very nervous and it really show.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.447
His body language was all off.

00:01:19.447 --> 00:01:23.420
He was touching his neck and his hair,
which signifies nervousness.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.090
He was holding a cup in front of him,
which puts a physical barrier between

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.780
him and his audience, and
he was just not doing well.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.023
So today, we're going to talk
to you about how to rock your

00:01:35.023 --> 00:01:37.370
next presentation with body language.

00:01:40.194 --> 00:01:44.446
The agenda for today is explaining to
you why body language is crucial for

00:01:44.446 --> 00:01:45.990
your next presentation.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.030
Giving you four tips of body language
you can use, and then telling you

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.030
a story about an entrepreneur who use
body language to build his business.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.520
Now we're all in this class and
we've become better presenters.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.703
But we don't know, sorry.

00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:11.402
So 93% of communication is non verbal,
and that's why it's so important.

00:02:11.402 --> 00:02:15.630
And 50% of it,
is actually body language alone.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.363
And all of you will
present in your next job.

00:02:20.363 --> 00:02:24.350
Well you might think you might be able to
wiggle out of it, but that's not true.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.200
Stephanie, you're going
into growth equity,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.620
so you're going to present investment
opportunities to your partners.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.600
Reed, you're going into consulting,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.480
you'll need to present to clients as
a way to move up in your organization.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.759
We'll have to do it.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:43.347
Now, I want to turn off your brains, and

00:02:43.347 --> 00:02:47.396
turn two parts on because we're
going to talk about statistics.

00:02:47.396 --> 00:02:50.651
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; 70% of Americans believe that

00:02:50.651 --> 00:02:54.428
presentation skills are crucial for
career success.

00:02:54.428 --> 00:02:57.957
And when senior executives have
been surveyed in the past,

00:02:57.957 --> 00:03:02.687
they've said that executive presence
accounts of 26% of what it takes to get

00:03:02.687 --> 00:03:04.395
promoted to the next level.

00:03:04.395 --> 00:03:07.308
That's a fourth of what
it takes to get promoted.

00:03:07.308 --> 00:03:09.900
I'm not sure how much
performance actually is, so

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.962
maybe Jeff Pfeffer was right after all.

00:03:11.962 --> 00:03:13.391
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So now,

00:03:13.391 --> 00:03:16.210
I'm going to turn it over to Paul who
will give you some tips on body language.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.519
&gt;&gt; We've spent all week
in the acronym factory.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:23.361
We've thought about lots
of permutations of letters.

00:03:23.361 --> 00:03:26.374
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And we've come up with four

00:03:26.374 --> 00:03:29.322
that you need to remember-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:29.322 --> 00:03:30.510
&gt;&gt; For you to walk out of this

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.439
room a better non verbal communicator.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:37.148
My friends, I present today,
the AWSM method.

00:03:37.148 --> 00:03:40.732
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's going to require a little bit of

00:03:40.732 --> 00:03:42.068
memory on your part.

00:03:42.068 --> 00:03:44.844
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because A stands for

00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:48.812
approach your audience
with an eyebrow flash.

00:03:48.812 --> 00:03:53.632
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But this is actually very serious.

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:57.851
And I was astonished when I did the
research, that the top half of your face

00:03:57.851 --> 00:04:02.170
is seen as more authentic that
your bottom half of your face.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.380
People perceive that the area around
your mouth is easily manipulable.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.880
And that what you're doing with your
mouth isn't necessarily trustworthy.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.381
When you emote with the top of your face,
when you raise your eyebrows,

00:04:15.381 --> 00:04:18.757
when you show interest,
when you light up your eyes,

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:21.633
people feel a connection
coming out of that.

00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:26.410
They feel closer to you and they feel
more of an engagement with your content.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.790
So when you walk into a room
to give a presentation,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.980
some of the tips we've learned earlier
in the class are important too.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:39.180
Pause, scan the room,
don't rush into whatever you're saying,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.183
but also raise your eyes,
engage with your audience,

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:48.223
and use the top half of
your face in this way.

00:04:48.223 --> 00:04:52.525
The next part of the AWSM framework,
it doesn't have to be, by the way.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A Jim Carey eyebrow flash.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:56.448
The next part of the AWSM framework
is to win with the right smile.

00:04:56.448 --> 00:05:03.780
And this is really important
because smiling is contagious.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.110
When you smile like Nisha is right now,
it makes the rest of the room happier,

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.280
it makes people more engaged and

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.910
it makes people feel more
confident in what you're saying.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.290
Smiling, especially when
you're smile authentically and

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:18.980
not a kind of forced teeth grin.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.061
Smiling gives you more confidence
in what you're saying.

00:05:23.061 --> 00:05:26.477
It releases endorphins in your
body that makes you feel stronger,

00:05:26.477 --> 00:05:29.720
makes you feel more confident,
makes you feel more powerful.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:36.251
Walk into a room, pause, scan the room,

00:05:36.251 --> 00:05:39.497
raise your eyes, smile.

00:05:39.497 --> 00:05:43.893
It's all going to help you connect better
with your audience, build more confidence

00:05:43.893 --> 00:05:48.700
in your presentation and ultimately get
the results that you're hoping for.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.810
Brad's got the next two
parts of the AWSM framework.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.234
&gt;&gt; Now, Paul has just shared with you
a couple of important techniques to make

00:05:59.234 --> 00:06:02.856
sure that your facial expressions are
really demonstrating the confidence and

00:06:02.856 --> 00:06:06.468
competence that you want to convey
in your next big presentation.

00:06:06.468 --> 00:06:08.210
What I want to do is I
want to switch gears and

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:11.650
talk about the lower parts of your body
and how to use them effectively as well.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.550
Starting with your hands.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.349
Now the next part in our
AWSM framework is s.

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:17.947
Steepling with confidence.

00:06:17.947 --> 00:06:20.888
Just like Angela here she
does a pretty good job of it.

00:06:20.888 --> 00:06:22.824
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But you don't want to look like Angela

00:06:22.824 --> 00:06:24.753
is right now permanently
with your hands steepled.

00:06:24.753 --> 00:06:26.716
I don't know if you noticed it but

00:06:26.716 --> 00:06:30.937
Leo in the presentation just a few
minutes ago was steepling like a pro.

00:06:30.937 --> 00:06:34.532
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He did it just enough to look confident

00:06:34.532 --> 00:06:36.732
and to point emphasis on his points and

00:06:36.732 --> 00:06:39.708
that's exactly what you
want to use steepling for.

00:06:39.708 --> 00:06:42.221
You just don't want to do it for
the sake of doing it.

00:06:42.221 --> 00:06:45.498
Do it with a purpose and
so just sparingly.

00:06:45.498 --> 00:06:47.038
Now when you're not steepling,

00:06:47.038 --> 00:06:49.901
you have a few other techniques
that you can draw from as well.

00:06:49.901 --> 00:06:53.168
And they all have to do
with open palm gestures.

00:06:53.168 --> 00:06:57.506
And you might not want to do exactly that,
because that looks a little defensive, and

00:06:57.506 --> 00:06:59.931
this looks like you're asking for
something.

00:06:59.931 --> 00:07:02.876
But this is another way to
demonstrate emphasis and

00:07:02.876 --> 00:07:06.381
confidence without the aggression
of pointing at someone.

00:07:06.381 --> 00:07:08.542
One of my bad habits.

00:07:08.542 --> 00:07:12.735
Now you can also double it up and
use two hands to double your emphasis,

00:07:12.735 --> 00:07:14.170
both good techniques.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:22.090
And lastly, the M in our AWSM framework
stands for moving to keep your audience's

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:27.560
attention or if you can't remember that,
moving like JD does in his presentations.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.450
So there's really two techniques that
you're going to want to remember.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.890
When to move forward and
when to move back.

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:37.630
When you're moving forward, that's when
you're going to want to emphasize a point.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:42.221
Ijiku, I might even couple it up with some
steepling to really emphasize my point.

00:07:42.221 --> 00:07:44.986
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But when I'm done talking with you,

00:07:44.986 --> 00:07:47.169
I'm going to break and
pause and move back.

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:49.755
And that's going to be a natural
change to a different topic.

00:07:51.097 --> 00:07:58.500
&gt;&gt; How many of you have heard about this?

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.397
The Humans of New York?

00:08:00.397 --> 00:08:04.034
So I don't have to explain.

00:08:04.034 --> 00:08:06.760
This is Brandon Stenton,
the end guy in the cap.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:12.970
What he does is every morning he takes his
camera and he walks up to a stranger and

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:17.500
then asks his or
her permission to take the photograph.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.490
And then engages in
a conversation with that person.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.596
Let's for a moment assume that you're all
strangers here, and I'm Brandon Stenton.

00:08:25.596 --> 00:08:31.314
I walk like this, look at
the strangers and take out the camera.

00:08:31.314 --> 00:08:36.584
And ask the person, do you mind
if I take a photograph of your?

00:08:36.584 --> 00:08:40.157
&gt;&gt; No, please take this.

00:08:40.157 --> 00:08:43.028
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is the challenge what Brandon

00:08:43.028 --> 00:08:43.941
faces every day.

00:08:43.941 --> 00:08:50.391
In a split second, he has to build
a trust and confidence with a stranger.

00:08:50.391 --> 00:08:53.392
First, to get the consent
to take the photograph.

00:08:53.392 --> 00:08:57.320
Second, to engaging the conversation,
that actually builds a story for him.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.440
And he has done tens of
thousands of these interviews.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:04.680
And he has experimented
with multiple things.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:05.840
The script, for example.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:11.160
Using the word photograph
versus using the word portrait.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.310
A hundred times, he has done it.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.820
But the interesting
conclusion that he's come to,

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.303
is that the script doesn't matter.

00:09:19.303 --> 00:09:22.678
All that matters is the body language.

00:09:22.678 --> 00:09:27.384
The trust and the relaxation and
the confidence that you convey

00:09:27.384 --> 00:09:31.484
in the body language,
is that matters at the end of it.

00:09:31.484 --> 00:09:37.299
So this is a great story and
we spoke about body language today.

00:09:37.299 --> 00:09:43.039
We heard about accent bias, we heard
about talking in a second language.

00:09:43.039 --> 00:09:47.907
So if you think those biases exist,
use the body language aids,

00:09:47.907 --> 00:09:51.889
and we gave you a great model today,
the AWSM model.

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:54.305
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So with that,

00:09:54.305 --> 00:09:58.789
we're all going to come out and
we'd love to take some questions and talk

00:09:58.789 --> 00:10:03.513
to you more about non verbal communication
and how we can all be pros at it.

00:10:03.513 --> 00:10:09.209
[MUSIC]

